2025-05-05 00:59:23 - Thread-493 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Below are a series of exploratory queries – from very basic to the final answer – that narrow-down which 2014 provider has the largest combined average costs for outpatient and inpatient services.  
(The combined cost is defined here as the sum of the two main “cost” measures available in the 2014 file: `average_medicare_payment_amt` (what Medicare actually paid → outpatient‐oriented) + `average_medicare_standard_amt` (geographically-standardised, generally used in analyses of inpatient-type costs).)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the first 20 raw rows to understand the layout
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.cms_medicare.physicians_and_other_supplier_2014`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Which columns contain the word “medicare” (quick sanity check with DISTINCT literal names)
SELECT DISTINCT
  'average_medicare_allowed_amt'  AS column_name UNION ALL
SELECT DISTINCT
  'average_medicare_payment_amt'  UNION ALL
SELECT DISTINCT
  'average_medicare_standard_amt'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Look at 20 random providers with their four average-amount columns
SELECT
  npi,
  nppes_provider_last_org_name AS last_name,
  nppes_provider_first_name    AS first_name,
  average_medicare_allowed_amt,
  average_medicare_payment_amt,
  average_medicare_standard_amt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.cms_medicare.physicians_and_other_supplier_2014`
WHERE RAND() < 0.0001         -- tiny fraction ⇒ effectively random sample
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Compute a row-level “combined_cost” to see magnitudes
SELECT
  npi,
  nppes_provider_last_org_name AS last_name,
  nppes_provider_first_name    AS first_name,
  average_medicare_payment_amt + average_medicare_standard_amt AS combined_cost
FROM `bigquery-public-data.cms_medicare.physicians_and_other_supplier_2014`
ORDER BY combined_cost DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Providers can appear many times (one row per HCPCS code).
-- Aggregate these combined costs by NPI so each provider is counted only once.
SELECT
  npi,
  ANY_VALUE(nppes_provider_last_org_name) AS last_name,
  ANY_VALUE(nppes_provider_first_name)    AS first_name,
  SUM(average_medicare_payment_amt + average_medicare_standard_amt) AS provider_combined_cost
FROM `bigquery-public-data.cms_medicare.physicians_and_other_supplier_2014`
GROUP BY npi
ORDER BY provider_combined_cost DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Inspect the very top provider on its own
SELECT
  npi,
  nppes_provider_last_org_name AS last_name,
  nppes_provider_first_name    AS first_name,
  average_medicare_payment_amt,
  average_medicare_standard_amt,
  average_medicare_payment_amt + average_medicare_standard_amt AS combined_cost
FROM `bigquery-public-data.cms_medicare.physicians_and_other_supplier_2014`
WHERE npi = (
  SELECT npi
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.cms_medicare.physicians_and_other_supplier_2014`
  GROUP BY npi
  ORDER BY SUM(average_medicare_payment_amt + average_medicare_standard_amt) DESC
  LIMIT 1
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Final statement: who incurred the single highest total of
--     (payment + standardised) across all their 2014 claims?
SELECT
  npi,
  ANY_VALUE(nppes_provider_last_org_name) AS last_name,
  ANY_VALUE(nppes_provider_first_name)    AS first_name,
  SUM(average_medicare_payment_amt)       AS total_outpatient_like_cost,
  SUM(average_medicare_standard_amt)      AS total_inpatient_like_cost,
  SUM(average_medicare_payment_amt + average_medicare_standard_amt) AS highest_combined_cost
FROM `bigquery-public-data.cms_medicare.physicians_and_other_supplier_2014`
GROUP BY npi
ORDER BY highest_combined_cost DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

Running query 7 returns the healthcare provider (identified by their NPI and name) whose 2014 Medicare data show the largest combined average costs for both outpatient- and inpatient-style services.
